TY  - JOUR
DB  - Ovid MEDLINE(R) <2008 to 2009>
ID  - 19219405
T1  - Comparison of pain and ecchymosis with low-molecular-weight heparin vs. unfractionated heparin in patients requiring bridging anticoagulation after warfarin interruption: a randomized trial.
T3  - Erratum in: J Thromb Thrombolysis. 2009 Oct;28(3):269 Note: McDonald, Ellen [added]
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1007/s11239-009-0312-8
A1  - Jamula, Erin
A1  - Woods, Karen
A1  - Verhovsek, Madeleine
A1  - Douketis, James D
A1  - McDonald, Ellen
Y1  - 2009//
Y2  - 20090214//
N1  - Erratum in (EIN)
N2  - BACKGROUND: Subcutaneous (SC) low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) or unfractionated heparin (UFH) are safe and efficacious for bridging anticoagulation after warfarin interruption. Although LMWH and UFH are self-administered by >90% of patients, factors that may be important to patients such as differences in pain and ecchymosis have not been explored., METHODS: We randomized 24 patients to receive SC LMWH or SC UFH twice-daily during the perioperative period. Injection associated pain was recorded using a visual analogue scale and area of ecchymosis was measured by digital photography of the injection site on the day of the procedure., RESULTS: The area of ecchymosis was 2-fold higher with UFH than LMWH (19.4 cm(2) vs. 8.98 cm(2); P = 0.33) and pain was similar with both treatments (115 mm vs. 171 mm; P = 0.25), though neither finding attained statistical significance., CONCLUSIONS: This exploratory study was underpowered to detect differences between the groups. Further studies are needed to reliably compare pain and ecchymosis in LMWH vs. UFH.
